Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 04:38:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 51/52] feat(mcp): screenshots at any scale, fitted to a size,
 or cropped to an element or region
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

get_frame_screenshot took scale 1 or 2 and always rendered the whole
frame. An image costs a model tokens by its pixel area, not its bytes
(Claude: about one token per 28×28 px, an image capped near 2,576 px on
the long edge), so the only ways to see more cheaply, or to see small
type at all, are fewer pixels or the right pixels:

- scale: any value from 0.1 to 2. A 1440×900 frame is ~1,700 tokens at
  1x and ~430 at 0.5, enough for a glance at the layout.
- max_size: the image's longest edge in px; lowers the scale to fit.
- element_id: crop to the box of the element with that data-doop-id
  (padding around it, default 16), measured in the rendered page.
- region: crop to a box in design px. A 1440×6000 page was shrunk by the
  model to about 620×2,576, too small for body text; four slices of
  1,600 px each are readable.
- format: png or jpeg. JPEG only makes the download smaller; the token
  cost is the same, which the description says.

renderFrame already took a fractional scale; renderFrameRegion adds the
crop, kept inside the frame (the canvas clips a frame to its size), and
returns the region it shows. The tool's text line names the region and
the image's px size, and tells the agent to read a tall frame in parts.
A crop naming nothing is the caller's error, said as is. Only
whole-frame PNGs at 1x or 2x prime the image cache, as before.

Tests: mcpScreenshotCrop.test.ts (real Chromium: sizes for scale,
max_size, element and region crops, JPEG, and the errors).
